* Models of the signal chain sheet that no other sheet uses, written for this
* project. None of these is a model of a manufacturer. Every figure is from
* the datasheet named beside it; a figure that is an assumption says so.

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------
* ADS8860 (U30): the analog input and the reference pin. Texas Instruments
* datasheet SBAS569B of February 2019.
*   Input: figure 45 (page 20) gives 4 pF at each input pin, then a
*   sampling switch of 96 ohm and a sampling capacitor of 55 pF to ground,
*   and protection diodes from each input to REF and to ground. The input
*   capacitance during acquisition is 59 pF (page 6).
*   Timing: the rising edge of the convert-start line opens the switch;
*   the conversion takes 500 ns to 710 ns (page 6) and the switch closes
*   again at its end (page 19). The model converts for tconv, 710 ns by
*   default, and expects the line to stay high for at least that long, as
*   the three-wire mode asks, and low for 150 ns or more before the next
*   rising edge, which empties the timer of the model.
*   What the sampling capacitor holds when it is reconnected is not in the
*   datasheet. The model shares its charge with a capacitor cres that is
*   empty at every conversion: 5.5 nF by default, which leaves 1 % and is
*   the bounding case of an empty capacitor. A smaller cres stands for a
*   converter that keeps part of the charge.
*   Reference pin: 300 uA during the conversion at mid-code with a
*   reference of 5 V, 250 nA of leakage otherwise (page 6, figure 67 on
*   page 37). The model draws iref during the conversion, in proportion to
*   the reference voltage (assumption: the charge of a capacitor array), as
*   a constant current; the real current comes as one packet per bit.
*   Protection diodes: an ordinary junction with a saturation current of
*   10 fA (assumption; the datasheet gives the rating of 0.3 V beyond REF
*   and ground and no curve).
*   Logic threshold of the convert-start pin: 1.5 V (assumption).
* What it leaves out: the conversion itself and its result, the digital
* pins (the block of the digital sheet has them), the input leakage of 5 nA,
* the dependence of the reference current on the code, the supply pins.
* Pins: REF, AINP, AINN, GND, CONVST.
.model SIGNAL_CHAIN_DESD D(IS=1e-14 N=1 RS=10)
.subckt SIGNAL_CHAIN_ADS8860 ref ainp ainn gnd cnv
+ params: csh=55p rsw=96 cpin=4p tconv=710n cres=5.5n iref=300u vth=1.5
.param goff=1e-12
.param gsw={1/rsw - 1e-12}
.param gres={1/50 - 1e-12}
* pins: capacitance and protection diodes
Cpp ainp gnd {cpin}
Cpn ainn gnd {cpin}
Dpu ainp ref SIGNAL_CHAIN_DESD
Dpd gnd ainp SIGNAL_CHAIN_DESD
Dnu ainn ref SIGNAL_CHAIN_DESD
Dnd gnd ainn SIGNAL_CHAIN_DESD
* convert-start line as a level between 0 and 1
Bc c0 0 V = 0.5*(1 + tanh((v(cnv,gnd) - {vth})/0.1))
Rc c0 c 1k
Cc c 0 1p
* conversion timer: a ramp that starts with the rising edge of the line,
* passes 1 after tconv and stops at 1.5. While the line is low it is
* emptied with 20 ns. A ramp and not a transmission line: the breakpoints
* of a line can stop a long run.
Ctm tm 0 1n
Rtm tm 0 1e9
Btm 0 tm I = v(c)*{1n/tconv}*min(max((1.5 - v(tm))*10, 0), 1) - (1 - v(c))*v(tm)*0.05
* converting: 1 from the rising edge until the ramp passes 1
Bq q 0 V = min(max(v(c)*0.5*(1 - tanh((v(tm) - 1)/0.003)), 0), 1)
* sampling switches, closed while the converter acquires. They are open
* before the charge of the capacitors is touched: the first half of the
* change of q opens them, the second half starts the sharing.
Bsp ainp shp I = v(ainp,shp)*({goff} + {gsw}*(1 - min(2*v(q), 1)))
Bsn ainn shn I = v(ainn,shn)*({goff} + {gsw}*(1 - min(2*v(q), 1)))
Cshp shp gnd {csh}
Cshn shn gnd {csh}
* the charge that a conversion takes from the sampling capacitors: shared
* with cres while converting, cres emptied while acquiring
Bxp shp rp I = v(shp,rp)*({goff} + {gres}*max(2*v(q) - 1, 0))
Bxn shn rn I = v(shn,rn)*({goff} + {gres}*max(2*v(q) - 1, 0))
Crp rp gnd {cres}
Crn rn gnd {cres}
Bep rp gnd I = v(rp,gnd)*({goff} + {gres}*(1 - min(2*v(q), 1)))
Ben rn gnd I = v(rn,gnd)*({goff} + {gres}*(1 - min(2*v(q), 1)))
* reference pin: leakage, and the current of a conversion
Rref ref gnd 2e7
Bref ref gnd I = {iref}*v(q)*v(ref,gnd)/5
.ends SIGNAL_CHAIN_ADS8860

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------
* MUX509 (U24) with one channel pair held closed, for noise runs. The model
* of the multiplexer in mux509.lib makes its channels from behavioral
* sources, which make no noise; here the closed channels are resistors, so
* that their thermal noise is in a noise analysis. The figures are those of
* that model: ron per channel (250 ohm by default, an estimate between the
* datasheet values for other supplies) and 6.7 pF at each output.
*   sel   the channel pair that is closed, 1 to 4 (range 0 to range 3)
*   Not modelled: switching, leakage, charge injection, the noise of the
*   channel beyond that of its resistance.
* Pins in the order of the package: A0 EN VSS S1A S2A S3A S4A DA DB S4B S3B
* S2B S1B VDD GND A1.
.subckt SIGNAL_CHAIN_MUX509_HELD a0 en vss s1a s2a s3a s4a da db s4b s3b s2b s1b vdd gnd a1
+ params: ron=250 roff=1e12 cdon=6.7p sel=1
R1a s1a da {sel == 1 ? ron : roff}
R2a s2a da {sel == 2 ? ron : roff}
R3a s3a da {sel == 3 ? ron : roff}
R4a s4a da {sel == 4 ? ron : roff}
R1b s1b db {sel == 1 ? ron : roff}
R2b s2b db {sel == 2 ? ron : roff}
R3b s3b db {sel == 3 ? ron : roff}
R4b s4b db {sel == 4 ? ron : roff}
Cda da gnd {cdon}
Cdb db gnd {cdon}
Ra0 a0 gnd 1e9
Ra1 a1 gnd 1e9
Ren en gnd 1e9
Rvd vdd gnd 1e9
Rvs vss gnd 1e9
.ends SIGNAL_CHAIN_MUX509_HELD

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------
* The OPA365 with the sources that measure the loop of the driver rail
* buffer U28: a voltage in series with its output and a current into its
* output pin, for the double injection method. With both amplitudes at 0
* the part is the amplifier alone. The first takes the model of opamps.lib,
* the second the model of the manufacturer, which has the same pin order.
*   vac   amplitude of the voltage in series with the amplifier output
*   iac   amplitude of the current into the output pin
.subckt SIGNAL_CHAIN_OPA365_PROBE inp inn vp vn out params: vac=0 iac=0 vos=0 ib=0.2p
X1 inp inn vp vn amp OPA365 vos={vos} ib={ib}
Vser amp out dc 0 ac {vac}
Iinj 0 out dc 0 ac {iac}
.ends SIGNAL_CHAIN_OPA365_PROBE

.subckt SIGNAL_CHAIN_OPA365X_PROBE inp inn vp vn out params: vac=0 iac=0
X1 inp inn vp vn amp OPA365
Vser amp out dc 0 ac {vac}
Iinj 0 out dc 0 ac {iac}
.ends SIGNAL_CHAIN_OPA365X_PROBE

* The OPA197 with the same sources, for the loop of the pedestal buffer U26.
.subckt SIGNAL_CHAIN_OPA197_PROBE inp inn vp vn out params: vac=0 iac=0 vos=0 ib=5p
X1 inp inn vp vn amp OPA197 vos={vos} ib={ib}
Vser amp out dc 0 ac {vac}
Iinj 0 out dc 0 ac {iac}
.ends SIGNAL_CHAIN_OPA197_PROBE
